The '''2026 AFA World Cup''' will be the 18th [[AFA World Cup]], a quadrennial [[W:List of association football competitions|international football tournament]] contested by the [[W:List of men's national association football teams|men's national teams]] of the member associations of AFA. It is scheduled to take place in [[Balisca]] and [[Sanrobonia]] from 14 June to 15 July 2026, after both countries were awarded the hosting rights on 5 January 2019. This will be the first World Cup ever held in two countries, and the first held in the Northern Hemisphere since 2006.

The final tournament will involve 32 national teams, which include 30 teams determined through qualifying competitions and the automatically qualified two host teams. A total of 64 matches will be played in 20 venues located in 20 cities. The opening ceremony will take place in [[Stadion Agung Martapura]], [[Martapura]], [[Sanrobonia]], on DATE, while the final will take place on 15 July in [[Castejón]] at the [[Estadio Olímpico]].

==Host selection==
Initially, [[Balisca]] and [[Sanrobonia]] submitted rival bids. Sanrobonia's entry into the race was seen by many observers as a direct response to the bid of its diplomatic and sporting rival Balisca — the two nations had been locked in a posture of mutual hostility since the [[Abyalan Cold War]], and the World Cup bid became another arena for their competition for regional influence and soft power. AFA leaders were split on whom to favour as host, as politics within the world governing body held sway.

The two rivals went on a massive and expensive PR blitz around the world. Balisca's bid, led by the [[Baliscan Football Federation]] (FFB) and backed by the [[Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation (Balisca)|Secretariat of International Affairs]], emphasised the confederation's world-class infrastructure, its experience hosting the [[2019 AFA Confederations Cup]], and the Silicon Coast's global brand recognition. Sanrobonia's bid, led by the [[Sanrobonian Football Association]] (PSSI) and backed personally by [[President of Sanrobonia|President]] [[TBA]], emphasised the nation's longstanding football culture, its global economic connections, and the symbolic power of bringing the World Cup to a country that had never before hosted a major AFA tournament.

AFA President [[Carlos Menéndez Vidal]] had long backed the Sanrobonian bid, viewing it as the safer and more commercially lucrative option. But his rival within AFA, [[Illypnian Football Confederation|Confederación Ilipnia de Fútbol]] (CIF) chief [[Yuri Kamarov]], sought to undermine Menéndez Vidal's plans by rallying support for the Baliscan bid — a move widely interpreted as an attempt to weaken Menéndez Vidal's grip on the presidency ahead of the 2020 AFA election. The geopolitical dimension further complicated the vote: the Fosian and Illypnian blocs voted heavily in favor of Sanrobonia, while the Lurandian, Kaftian and Abyalan member associations — excluding [[Potocsí]] — lined up behind Balisca.

The 210-member AFA convened in [[Mazar]], [[Burawa]] on 2 December 2018 to vote to select the hosts. On the first round of balloting, the two nations ended up deadlocked at 103 votes each, with four members abstaining from the vote. The deadlock exposed the depth of the rift within AFA and raised the prospect of a protracted and politically damaging stalemate. Menéndez Vidal, recognising that neither bid could achieve a majority without a bruising fight that would damage AFA's credibility, proposed a compromise: a joint bid. The CIF and the Abyalan football associations, viewing co-hosting between the two Abyalan rivals as the best available option, pressured both bidders to accept.

Balisca and Sanrobonia were finally faced with a choice of having no World Cup or a shared World Cup, and they reluctantly chose to go along with co-hosting. The decision was deeply unpopular in both countries.

